I pushed a follow-up to refine the HTTP backend feature model from the earlier review discussion. Rather than removing reqwest, the implementation now allows bitreq and reqwest to coexist as additive features, with reqwest taking precedence when both are enabled because it supports the fuller set of behavior, including SOCKS proxies and invalid-certificate handling. bitreq remains the lightweight default backend for native builds, but its more limited proxy support is now validated explicitly so unsupported configurations fail clearly instead of behaving inconsistently. I also tightened the related backend behavior by preserving no-redirect handling, improving WASM request error handling, and simplifying the request builder API by moving the fluent methods onto inherent implementations instead of requiring an extension trait import.

I think we can clean up these features a bit better in a follow up PR by splitting the wallet and mint into separate crates. 

