# Adding an Iroh (QUIC p2p) transport

If this PR gets merged this is how easy it would be to add [Iroh](https://iroh.computer) instead of HTTP (compared to https://github.com/cashubtc/cdk/pull/2285)

## The seam

Read [ADR](https://github.com/crodas/cdk/blob/a2f6850c926c00c9e3c2fcc82d3cb3f91763ca0c/docs/adr/0004-mint-server-transport-seam.md) first. A transport is a byte channel plus a codec; the mint RPCs already exist as two mirror traits:

- client: `cdk::wallet::MintConnector` (`crates/cdk/src/wallet/mint_connector/mod.rs`)
- server: `cdk::mint::MintServer` (`crates/cdk/src/mint/server.rs`), implemented
  by `Mint`

Both speak the NUT request/response structs (`SwapRequest`, `MintResponse`, ...), which are already `serde`. So Iroh only needs to carry those structs as JSON and dispatch them; no HTTP.

## Wire framing

Unary RPC uses one length-delimited envelope per request, one QUIC bidi stream per call (QUIC streams are cheap):

```jsonc
// request envelope (transport-defined wrapper)
{ "auth": "<NUT-22 token, omitted if none>", "request": <MintRpcRequest> }
// reply
{ "ok": <value> }               // or
{ "err": <ErrorResponse> }      // cdk::error::ErrorResponse (NUT-00)
```

`request` is `cdk::rpc::MintRpcRequest` (an adjacently-tagged enum, one variant per `MintServer` method, serialized `{"method":..,"params":..}`) and the reply is `cdk::rpc::MintRpcResponse`. The `method` tag is the analog of the HTTP route path; the server dispatches with `cdk::rpc::dispatch` and gates auth with
`MintRpcRequest::protected_endpoint()`.

`cdk::rpc::MintRpcRequest` deliberately carries no auth, so the NUT-22 token rides in a thin transport wrapper alongside it (`auth` is a `cdk::nuts::AuthToken`; each protected request needs a fresh one):

```rust
#[derive(Serialize, Deserialize)]
struct Envelope { auth: Option<AuthToken>, request: MintRpcRequest }
```

NUT-17 subscriptions use `open_stream`: a dedicated bidi stream whose frames are the raw `String` NUT-17 messages, fed straight into `StreamTx`/`StreamRx` (`cdk::stream_channel`).

## Client: `impl MintConnector`

```rust
pub struct IrohClient { conn: iroh::endpoint::Connection }

impl IrohClient {
    // Pin the mint's NodeId; the QUIC handshake authenticates it (no MITM).
    pub async fn connect(endpoint: &iroh::Endpoint, node: iroh::NodeAddr) -> Result<Self> {
        let conn = endpoint.connect(node, b"cashu/mint/1").await?;
        Ok(Self { conn })
    }
    // Byte roundtrip over one QUIC stream; JSON in, MintRpcResponse out.
    // The per-request NUT-22 token is minted from the auth wallet keyed on the
    // request's endpoint, exactly like HttpClient does.
    async fn rpc(&self, req: MintRpcRequest) -> Result<MintRpcResponse, Error> {
        let auth = match (req.protected_endpoint(), self.get_auth_wallet().await) {
            (Some(ep), Some(w)) => w.get_auth_for_request(&ep).await?,
            _ => None,
        };
        let (mut send, mut recv) = self.conn.open_bi().await?;
        send.write_all(&serde_json::to_vec(&Envelope { auth, request: req })?).await?;
        send.finish()?;
        Ok(serde_json::from_slice(&recv.read_to_end(1 << 20).await?)?)
    }
}

#[async_trait]
impl MintConnector for IrohClient {
    async fn post_swap(&self, r: SwapRequest) -> Result<SwapResponse, Error> {
        self.rpc(MintRpcRequest::PostSwap(r)).await?.decode()
    }
    // ... one line per method ...

    async fn open_stream(&self) -> Result<(StreamTx, StreamRx), Error> {
        let (send, recv) = self.conn.open_bi().await?;
        // LengthDelimitedCodec over the QUIC stream; map frames Bytes <-> String.
        let framed_tx = FramedWrite::new(send, LengthDelimitedCodec::new());
        let framed_rx = FramedRead::new(recv, LengthDelimitedCodec::new());
        Ok((
            StreamTx::new(framed_tx.with(|s: String| ready(Ok(Bytes::from(s))))),
            StreamRx::new(framed_rx.map(|f| Ok(String::from_utf8_lossy(&f?).into_owned()))),
        ))
    }
}
```

Wire it into a wallet with `WalletBuilder::shared_client(Arc::new(iroh_client))`. Everything above `MintConnector` (`Wallet`, sagas, `SubscriptionManager`) is unchanged.

## Server adapter

```rust
pub async fn serve(mint: Arc<Mint>, endpoint: iroh::Endpoint) {
    while let Some(incoming) = endpoint.accept().await {
        let mint = mint.clone();
        tokio::spawn(async move {
            let conn = incoming.await?;
            loop {
                let (mut send, mut recv) = conn.accept_bi().await?;   // one per request
                let Envelope { auth, request } =
                    serde_json::from_slice(&recv.read_to_end(1 << 20).await?)?;
                // Auth: dispatch does NO auth. Gate here, like cdk-axum's handlers.
                if let Some(ep) = request.protected_endpoint() {
                    mint.verify_auth(auth, &ep).await?;   // auth: Option<AuthToken>
                }
                let reply = cdk::rpc::dispatch(&*mint, request).await;
                send.write_all(&serde_json::to_vec(&reply)?).await?;
                send.finish()?;
            }
        });
    }
}
```

The NUT-17 stream ALPN (or a `subscribe` method) wraps the accepted bidi stream into `StreamTx`/`StreamRx` (`cdk::stream_channel`) and hands it to `mint.serve_stream(tx, rx).await`, which runs the subscription protocol until the stream closes.

## Addressing, discovery, security

- Address = `NodeId` + a `NodeAddr` (relay url and/or direct socket addrs). Publish it out of band, in the mint's `/v1/info`, in a pkarr/DNS record, or via the n0 discovery service. No hostname or certificate authority.
- Confidentiality and mint identity come from QUIC: the client pins the expected  `NodeId`, so it cannot be MITM'd. NUT-22 blind-auth rides in the transport  envelope (the `auth` field alongside the request) and is checked by  `verify_auth` server-side; QUIC authenticates the mint, auth authenticates the
  client.

## Crate layout

`cdk-iroh`: `IrohClient` (`MintConnector`) and `serve(Arc<Mint>, Endpoint)`. Deps: `iroh`, `tokio-util` (`LengthDelimitedCodec`), `serde_json`, and `cdk` (for `cdk::rpc`, `cdk::stream_channel`, and `cdk::nuts::AuthToken`). The server mirrors `cdk_axum::create_mint_router(Arc<Mint>, ...)`: it only needs an `Arc<Mint>`.

## Prerequisites in `cdk` (state of play)

Client wrap and server *unary* wrap need no `cdk` change (`MintConnector` + `shared_client`; `MintServer` + `Mint::verify_auth` on an embedded `Arc<Mint>`). For server streaming:

- **G1 (done): `Mint::serve_stream(&self, StreamTx, StreamRx)`** runs the NUT-17 protocol on caller-owned halves. The adapter wraps its bidi stream and calls it.
- **G2 (done): `cdk::stream_channel`** re-exports `StreamTx`/`StreamRx`/`StreamError`/`from_ws`/`in_memory_pair`, so a transport crate embeds `cdk`  alone.
- **G3 (done): shared dispatch table.** `cdk::rpc` provides `MintRpcRequest`, `MintRpcResponse` (+ `decode`), `MintRpcRequest::protected_endpoint`, and  `cdk::rpc::dispatch`, so Iroh, Enclavia, and gRPC share one method<->tag mapping and one server dispatch.

Not a gap: blind-auth (NUT-22) stays off HTTP by overriding the default`auth_connector` with a transport-native `AuthMintConnector`; OIDC and LNURL are inherently HTTP and stay on HTTP.

Thank you - targets https://github.com/cashubtc/cdk/issues/2366 very well.

It lets Minibits Watchdog to attribute a deposit to its mint quote directly + per-output `amount_sat` helps to filter-out sub mint's minimal onchain mint amounts.

While you are at it, I identified another extension of transaction data with high analogy:
add WalletTransactionInput inputs alongside outputs.

Use case:
I see sub-limit deposits to the cdk onchain wallet already happening. Those might be harmless, owever they might be easily used for clustering analysis once they become inputs into mint melts. This is what my separate issue (https://github.com/cashubtc/cdk/issues/2365) has an intention to cover.

Adding them to transaction list allows to set any kind of monitoring on them in the meantime.

### Proposed shape

Additive, mirroring `WalletTransactionOutput`:

```proto
message WalletTransactionInput {
    // Previous outpoint spent by this input.
    string txid = 1;
    uint32 vout = 2;
    // Value of the previous output, when known to the wallet.
    optional uint64 amount_sat = 3;
    // Address of the previous output, when it belongs to the wallet.
    optional string address = 4;
}

message WalletTransaction {
    // ...
    // Inputs spent by this transaction, in input order.
    repeated WalletTransactionInput inputs = 10;
}
```

`txid` and `vout` alone would be sufficient for us — matching input outpoints against
known uncredited deposit txids is all the detection needs. The amount and address fields
are conveniences.

This should not require new persistence: the wallet's own transaction already carries
`input[].previous_output`, which is the same source `outputs` is derived from.

Should we mirror the existing `PreparedSend` options pattern here rather than adding signing keys as a required positional argument?

[`prepare_send`](https://github.com/cashubtc/cdk/blob/main/crates/cdk/src/wallet/send/mod.rs#L159-L176) accepts [`SendOptions`](https://github.com/cashubtc/cdk/blob/main/crates/cdk-common/src/wallet/mod.rs#L450-L470), which carries externally supplied signing keys and other preparation settings. Its confirmation flow combines those keys with keys found in the wallet through [`merge_keyring_keys`](https://github.com/cashubtc/cdk/blob/main/crates/cdk/src/wallet/send/saga/mod.rs#L146-L168).

The melt implementation already performs the same local-key lookup with [`get_signing_key`](https://github.com/cashubtc/cdk/blob/b5b614b7abb1e4260c326e02066984bda6bed967/crates/cdk/src/wallet/melt/saga/mod.rs#L522-L535), so callers only need to supply keys for external proofs whose keys are not stored by the wallet.

Could we introduce a `MeltPrepareOptions` containing `metadata`, `p2pk_signing_keys`, and HTLC `preimages`, and add `prepare_melt_*_with_options` methods while retaining the existing methods as default wrappers? This would:

- keep `PreparedSend` and `PreparedMelt` APIs consistent;
- avoid adding another required positional argument;
- support HTLC preimages, as [`ReceiveOptions`](https://github.com/cashubtc/cdk/blob/main/crates/cdk-common/src/wallet/mod.rs#L522-L533) already does;
- allow signing and verification during confirmation, when `SIG_ALL` change outputs exist.

The options should remain in the in-memory `PreparedMelt` and not be persisted, so this should not require a database migration.
